==== New Lankh
<new-lankh>
- Languages: Mortanian, Psuedophidian
- 2 skill points in Alchemy, Devicecraft, Stealth, Exotic Arms
- Maintenance of cost of Lankh technology is reduced by 1

New Lankh is a soot-filled city which teleported from the Wandering Moon
twenty years ago, home to technological marvels that are slowly breaking
down in their new environment. Previously Lankh was merely a district of
a sprawling gaslit metropolis, ruled by vampiric overlords. Now its
position in an entirely new world is much more uncertain. For more on
their history see the \[\[Game Rules/Classes and Character
Creation/Cultural Background/Secrets of New Lankh|Secrets of New
Lankh\]\].

==== Blemmyes
<blemmyes>
- Languages: Blem, Ophidian
- 2 skill points in Bushcraft, Heal, Lore, Investigation
- Headless: 50% chance for any critical hit to be a regular hit instead

Headless men, also called the akephaloi, they have large faces on their
chests rather than the typical arrangement. Most live in and around
Blem, although some have migrated through Alcheran lands. The Blemmyes
claim descent from the original inhabitants of the Shrinelands, having
survived the floods of the First Age, corrupting sands of the Third Age,
and all number of wars, plagues, and disasters.

==== Ophidian
<ophidian>
- Languages: Ophidian, Psuedophidian, High Ophidian
- 2 skill points in Alchemy, Diplomacy, Language, Seneschal
- Toxic blood, ability to draw out poison

The height of the Ophidian Empire has long gone, and recent years have
added further strife. Civil wars rage across what was once the empire,
resulting in a small group of Ophidian refugees forming a sister city to
Blem. Ophidians themselves have a varying degree of serpentine traits.
Most slither on a serpentine tail, while all of them have poison blood
and fangs.

==== City of Miners
<city-of-miners>
- Languages: Underspeech, Alcheran
- 2 skill points in Artisanry, Devicecraft, Hand Weapon, Enchantment
- Cave sense: able to navigate in enclosed stone environments without
  light

The City of Miners has a distinct history harkening back to dwarven
holds from long ago. From this they still retain their traditions, and
the ability to thrive underground. In the time since they have become
more akin to their Alcheran neighbors as one of the great city-states.

==== Xanthian
<xanthian>
- Languages: Xanthian, Psuedophidian
- 2 skill points in Archery, Athletics, Sail, Teach
- Specializations in Archery (Great Bow) and Athletics (Swim)
- Gain 1 additional hit point per hit die

The storm-hardened shores of Xanthe are home to lands of incredible
abundance. Along these live the Xanthe, peerless sailors and travelers.
They live in wandering clan-ships, variously warring with rivals or
marrying friends. Greatest among them are their elders, those who have
survived a terrible transformation to become guides for their people -
however in recent times a corrupt king has taken power of exiled many of
the formerly esteemed elders. The Xanthe who have ended up in the
Shrinelands have a more tragic history, as the remnants of a shipwrecked
expedition stranded by a formidable storm.

==== Distant Realm
<distant-realm>
- Languages: native language of your homeland
- 2 skill points in Language, and any 3 General \[\[Game
  Rules/Skills|Skills\]\]
- +2 to a single ability score (maximum 18) reflecting the special
  nature of your original homeland

You are from a far distant realm, one unknown to most people of the
Shrinelands. Possibly even from another plane of existence, such
interlopers are common in land of Noachis and often find themselves
drawn to adventure in these western lands. Work with the GM to come up
with the exact nature of this origin.

Your starting 2 points in Language must be used to have an understanding
of Psuedophidian. Choose one of the above cultures for where your recent
primary residence has been, write this down alongside some details of
your original homeland. Decide if you have arrived alone, in which case
you live with some form of adopted family or community, or if a small
enclave of your people have joined the Shrinelands.

=== Time of Birth
<time-of-birth>
The adventure begins in the year 320. Choose what year your character
was born in, and the wind that presided over their birth. For those born
in a "short year", anytime between the year 300 and 278, these are: -
Zephyrus, law season, a temperate season with many festivals - Boreas,
monsoon season, a time of potent storms and torrential rain - Eurus,
chaos season, the most powerful season of vibrant growth and
unprecedented change - Notus, dry season, a dangerous time of oppressive
heat and war

For those born in the year 301 or after their birth occurred during a
long year. This is the result of the Wandering Moon, a sister planet
which arrives every 22 years. Each convergence results in an alteration
between short years and long years. Long years proceed slower, having
more days and an additional season: a cold and cursed time called Winter
\- the still season.

=== Class
<class>
Choose what class your character is.

The classes are fighting-men, men of many ways, and magic-users.
\[\[Game Rules/Classes and Character
Creation/Classes/Fighting-Man|Fighting-Men\]\] are masters at arms, and
able to lead with great skill. \[\[Game Rules/Classes and Character
Creation/Classes/Man of Many Ways|Men of Many Ways\]\] gain
specializations in all of their skills, and oft find themselves
protected by good fortune. Although any dedicated practitioner can learn
some sorcery, \[\[Game Rules/Classes and Character
Creation/Classes/Magic-User|Magic-Users\]\] have a natural affinity for
the arcane. Alternatively you may choose two half-classes, as detailed
under the rules for \[\[Game Rules/Classes and Character
Creation/Classes/Specialists/Dual-Class|Dual-Class\]\] characters.

For your class write down - Starting HP, this is the maximum value on
the HD + Constitution modifier - Saving throws, reflex includes
Dexterity modifier, mental includes Wisdom modifier, physical includes
Constitution modifier - Special abilities for your class

Additionally your characters have had some time as adventurers to gain a
variety of skills. For the specific classes you gain. Alternatively you
may choose a preset \[\[Game Rules/Classes and Character Creation/Class
Kits|Class Kits\]\] for a character with more specific training.

==== Fighting-Man
<fighting-man>
- Distribute 18 additional skill points (maximum of an additional 5 in
  any single skill)
- Heroic expertise in Martial Training

==== Men of Many Ways
<men-of-many-ways>
- Distribute 24 additional skill points (maximum of any additional 6 in
  any single skill, ignore bonus from Quick Study during character
  creation - this is factored in as the additional skill points)
- Heroic expertise in any General Expertise

==== Magic-User
<magic-user>
- Distribute 4 additional skill points to any General or Martial skills
- Distribute 12 skill points among magical skills
- Distribute 3 points among your elemental attunements: Air, Earth,
  Fire, Water, Celestial (maximum 2 in any single element)

Begin with some knowledge of the Curriculum, a set of spells developed
by the legendary Ecnavian wizards. For each magical skill you have and
each element you have learned the corresponding spell. Write these down
under mastered spells.

